@startuml
!include ../../../tools/diagrams/cka-theme.puml
skinparam sequence {
  LifeLineBorderColor #94A3B8
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #EFF6FF
  ParticipantBorderColor #326CE5
  ParticipantFontStyle bold
  DatabaseBackgroundColor #F1F5F9
}
title How the scheduler "monitors" the API server: a watch, not polling and not etcd

participant "kube-scheduler" as s
participant "kube-apiserver\n(watch cache)" as a
database "etcd" as e

== on startup ==
s -> a : LIST pods?fieldSelector=spec.nodeName=""\n(HTTPS, authenticated with scheduler.conf)
a --> s : current unscheduled Pods
s -> a : WATCH pods?fieldSelector=spec.nodeName=""\n(one long-lived connection)
a -> e : (API server has its own watch on etcd)

== a Pod is created ==
e --> a : change event
a --> s : watch event: ADDED pod web-1
s -> s : filter + score nodes
s -> a : POST pods/web-1/binding\n{ target: node01 }
a -> e : store spec.nodeName=node01
a --> s : 201 Created

caption <i>Every controller, the kubelet and kube-proxy follow the same list-then-watch pattern against the API server. None of them can reach etcd.</i>
@enduml
